            "authorAssociation": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> I also use dropbar.nvim, and I'm wondering if there is any way to zoom into the buffer but maintain my custom winbar? It really helps me to have it there to remember what file I'm working in. I will add that it reappears if I hit space.\r\n\r\nOf course, it requires writing a custom function wrapper. Something like this:\r\n\r\n```lua\r\nlocal zoom_with_winbar = function()\r\n  MiniMisc.zoom()\r\n  -- Set local winbar only if zoomed in\r\n  if vim.api.nvim_win_get_config(0).relative == '' then return end\r\n  vim.wo.winbar = 'my winbar'\r\nend\r\nvim.keymap.set('n', '<Leader>oz', zoom_with_winbar, { desc = 'Zoom window' })\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nAs this is not the first time I had to check if `zoom()` actually zoomed in or out, I wonder if it should return a boolean indicating so... :thinking: ",
            "bodyHTML": "<blockquote>\n<p dir=\"auto\">I also use dropbar.nvim, and I'm wondering if there is any way to zoom into the buffer but maintain my custom winbar? It really helps me to have it there to remember what file I'm working in. I will add that it reappears if I hit space.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Of course, it requires writing a custom function wrapper. Something like this:</p>\n<div class=\"highlight highlight-source-lua notranslate position-relative overflow-auto\" dir=\"auto\" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content=\"local zoom_with_winbar = function()\n  MiniMisc.zoom()\n  -- Set local winbar only if zoomed in\n  if vim.api.nvim_win_get_config(0).relative == '' then return end\n  vim.wo.winbar = 'my winbar'\nend\nvim.keymap.set('n', '&lt;Leader&gt;oz', zoom_with_winbar, { desc = 'Zoom window' })\"><pre class=\"notranslate\"><span class=\"pl-k\">local</span> <span class=\"pl-en\">zoom_with_winbar</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">=</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">function</span>()\n  <span class=\"pl-smi\">MiniMisc</span>.<span class=\"pl-c1\">zoom</span>()\n  <span class=\"pl-c\"><span class=\"pl-c\">--</span> Set local winbar only if zoomed in</span>\n  <span class=\"pl-k\">if</span> <span class=\"pl-smi\">vim</span>.<span class=\"pl-e\">api</span>.<span class=\"pl-c1\">nvim_win_get_config</span>(<span class=\"pl-c1\">0</span>).<span class=\"pl-e\">relative</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">==</span> <span class=\"pl-s\"><span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span><span class=\"pl-pds\">' </span></span><span class=\"pl-k\">then</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">return</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">end</span>\n  <span class=\"pl-smi\">vim</span>.<span class=\"pl-e\">wo</span>.<span class=\"pl-e\">winbar</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">=</span> <span class=\"pl-s\"><span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span>my winbar<span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span></span>\n<span class=\"pl-k\">end</span>\n<span class=\"pl-smi\">vim</span>.<span class=\"pl-e\">keymap</span>.<span class=\"pl-c1\">set</span>(<span class=\"pl-s\"><span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span>n<span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span></span>, <span class=\"pl-s\"><span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span>&lt;Leader&gt;oz<span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span></span>, <span class=\"pl-smi\">zoom_with_winbar</span>, { <span class=\"pl-smi\">desc</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">=</span> <span class=\"pl-s\"><span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span>Zoom window<span class=\"pl-pds\">' </span></span>})</pre></div>\n<hr>\n<p dir=\"auto\">As this is not the first time I had to check if <code class=\"notranslate\">zoom()</code> actually zoomed in or out, I wonder if it should return a boolean indicating so... 🤔</p>",
            "bodyText": "I also use dropbar.nvim, and I'm wondering if there is any way to zoom into the buffer but maintain my custom winbar? It really helps me to have it there to remember what file I'm working in. I will add that it reappears if I hit space.\n\nOf course, it requires writing a custom function wrapper. Something like this:\nlocal zoom_with_winbar = function()\n  MiniMisc.zoom()\n  -- Set local winbar only if zoomed in\n  if vim.api.nvim_win_get_config(0).relative == '' then return end\n  vim.wo.winbar = 'my winbar'\nend\nvim.keymap.set('n', '<Leader>oz', zoom_with_winbar, { desc = 'Zoom window' })\n\nAs this is not the first time I had to check if zoom() actually zoomed in or out, I wonder if it should return a boolean indicating so... 🤔",
